---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { flowControl, fieldInference, tmlrVenue, tmlrStatus } from '../data/research';
import PageIntro from '../components/PageIntro.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import { links } from '../data/site';
---

<BaseLayout title="Research" description="Ruige Kong's research in reliable surrogate modeling, learning-based flow control, scientific machine learning, and wildfire prediction." image="/images/cylinder-mechanism.png">
  <PageIntro eyebrow="Research" title="Reliable learning for physical dynamics and control." description="I study when neural surrogates preserve the state evolution, local sensitivities, and closed-loop behavior required for model-based control." />

  <section class="research-lead shell" aria-label="Research agenda">
    <div class="research-question">
      <p>A central question is when a learned dynamics model is reliable enough for controller design.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="research-agenda">
      <p>Low state-prediction error is not sufficient for control. A useful surrogate must also preserve action-conditioned dynamics and the local sensitivities used by an optimizer or policy.</p>
      <p>I study these requirements through neural operators, model-based reinforcement learning, direct numerical simulation, and controlled perturbation tests.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="project-deep-dive section" id="audited-surrogates">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="project-header">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">{tmlrStatus}</p>
          <h2>{flowControl.title}</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="project-collab">With <a href={links.fresca} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Stefania Fresca</a><br />University of Washington</p>
      </div>

      <p class="pub-summary">Workshop version: {flowControl.workshopStatus} to the {flowControl.workshopVenue}. Journal manuscript in preparation for {tmlrVenue}.</p>

      <div class="project-body-grid">
        <div class="project-statement">
          <p class="eyebrow">The question</p>
          <h3>Accurate state prediction does not imply a faithful control Jacobian.</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Neural surrogates can reduce the cost of trajectory optimization, but controller design depends on derivatives with respect to the action. These derivatives can be wrong even when one-step state errors are small.</p>
          <p>I compare automatic-differentiation gradients from the surrogate with central finite-difference sensitivities from a high-fidelity solver on held-out states. A compact feedback law fitted to the measured gradient field initializes a neural policy, and an anchor penalty keeps optimization close to that law. Controllers are evaluated in the original solver across four test cases.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <figure class="study-figure study-figure-wide">
        <img src="/images/surrogate-horizon.png" alt="Physical flow fields, surrogate predictions, and errors shown at the initial state, training boundary, and a later diagnostic time" width="1700" height="1403" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Teacher-forced predictions remain accurate near the training horizon, while the autoregressive rollout later diverges from DNS. The distinction bounds which dynamical claims the surrogate can support.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="method-steps" aria-label="Research workflow">
        <div><span>01</span><h4>Perturb</h4><p>Estimate action sensitivities with paired finite-difference DNS trajectories.</p></div>
        <div><span>02</span><h4>Audit</h4><p>Compare gradient sign, direction, and magnitude across relevant horizons.</p></div>
        <div><span>03</span><h4>Distill</h4><p>Fit a compact feedback law to the measured high-fidelity gradient field.</p></div>
        <div><span>04</span><h4>Refine</h4><p>Initialize a neural policy from the feedback law and keep it close with an anchor penalty.</p></div>
        <div><span>05</span><h4>Validate</h4><p>Evaluate closed-loop performance and falsification controls in DNS.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div class="figure-with-context">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Physical test</p>
          <h3>The audited gradient identifies a useful feedback phase.</h3>
          <p>The local action gradient predicts a feedback phase that lowers the objective in direct numerical simulation. A falsification phase produces the opposite response, providing a controlled test of the inferred mechanism.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="study-figure">
          <img src="/images/controller-phase.png" alt="A phase sweep and closed-loop physical simulation comparing a controller extracted from audited gradients with a falsification phase" width="1600" height="1588" loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>The phase selected from the audited gradient lowers the physical objective over the evaluation horizon.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <div class="project-note">
        <span class="note-label">Research principle</span>
        <p>Prediction accuracy, action-gradient fidelity, and closed-loop performance are distinct properties. An audit is local to the sampled states and actions, so gradients must also be measured around the actions the deployed controller applies.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <section class="section section-tint" id="scientific-ml">
    <div class="shell topic-layout">
      <div class="topic-index">02</div>
      <div class="topic-title">
        <p class="eyebrow">{tmlrStatus}</p>
        <h2>{fieldInference.title}</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="topic-copy prose">
        <p>When can a longer sensor history compensate for a smaller spatial observation window? I study this trade-off through exact Gaussian conditioning and learned local models on a forced linear field, viscous Burgers flow, and the chaotic Kuramoto–Sivashinsky equation.</p>
        <p>Noisy or sparse observations alone do not force a predictor to use distant sensors: spatial correlation in the underlying field matters. How much history can replace spatial context depends on the window size, sensor spacing, transport, and forecast lead time. In the tested Kuramoto–Sivashinsky regimes, history becomes more useful at longer lead times for windows up to one correlation length, a pattern absent from the tested Burgers regimes.</p>
        <ul class="inline-topics">
          <li>Partial observations</li><li>Temporal memory</li><li>Spatial context</li><li>PDE forecasting</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section shell wildfire-study" id="wildfire">
    <div class="wildfire-header">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Environmental prediction</p>
        <h2>Interpretable deep learning for wildfire spread</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>We trained Autoencoder, ResNet, U-Net, and Swin-UNet architectures for next day fire-mask segmentation using nearly a decade of remote sensing data from California.</p>
        <p>We evaluated predictive skill under class imbalance, computational cost, and data efficiency. SHAP, Grad-CAM, and Integrated Gradients were used to compare global and spatial feature attribution.</p>
        <div class="inline-links">
          <a class="text-link" href={links.wildfire} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the paper <Icon name="external" size={16} /></a>
          <a class="text-link" href={links.wildfireCode} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Code and data <Icon name="external" size={16} /></a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <figure class="study-figure wildfire-dataset-figure">
      <img src="/images/wildfire-dataset.png" alt="Five remote sensing samples with twelve input fields, a previous fire mask, and the next day fire mask" width="1600" height="800" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>Each row is one sample. The inputs include weather, terrain, vegetation, population density, energy release, and the previous fire mask. The final column is the next day fire mask. Figure from the published paper.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="wildfire-result-grid">
      <figure class="study-figure">
        <img src="/images/wildfire-shap.png" alt="SHAP feature importance rankings for XGBoost, Autoencoder, ResNet, U-Net, and Swin-UNet wildfire models" width="1400" height="1439" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Mean absolute SHAP values show that each architecture relies on a different mix of environmental inputs. Figure from the published paper.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="wildfire-findings">
        <p class="eyebrow">Main results</p>
        <h3>U-Net and Swin-UNet achieved the highest AUC-PR.</h3>
        <p>Swin-UNet reached a mean AUC-PR of 0.2803 and U-Net reached 0.2739. Both outperformed the Autoencoder and ResNet baselines on the highly imbalanced segmentation task.</p>
        <p>The attribution profiles differed despite similar aggregate performance. U-Net emphasized population density and the previous fire mask, while Swin-UNet emphasized vegetation, humidity, and the previous fire mask.</p>
        <p>The result shows why predictive metrics and attribution analysis should be considered together when comparing architectures.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="confidential-note shell" aria-label="Additional research">
    <p class="eyebrow">Additional research</p>
    <h2>Machine learning for satellite dynamics</h2>
    <p>I also contribute to the FISICA project on machine learning for satellite dynamics. Further details are limited due to confidentiality.</p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
